{16^2 \\cdot 3} = 16\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute this back into the expression:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{32 + 16\\sqrt{3}}\n\\]\n\nWe need to simplify \\(\\sqrt{32 + 16\\sqrt{3}}\\). Assume it can be expressed as \\(\\sqrt{x} + \\sqrt{y}\\) where \\(x\\) and \\(y\\) are integers. Then:\n\n\\[\n(\\sqrt{x} + \\sqrt{y})^2 = x + y + 2\\sqrt{xy}\n\\]\n\nWe need:\n\n\\[\nx + y = 32 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 2\\sqrt{xy} = 16\\sqrt{3}\n\\]\n\nFrom \\(2\\sqrt{xy} = 16\\sqrt{3}\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{xy} = 8\\sqrt{3} \\implies xy = 192\n\\]\n\nWe now solve the system of equations:\n\n\\[\nx + y = 32\n\\]\n\\[\nxy = 192\n\\]\n\nThese are the roots of the quadratic equation:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - 32t + 192 = 0\n\\]\n\nUsing the quadratic formula \\(t = \\frac{32 \\pm \\sqrt{32^2 - 4 \\cdot 192}}{2}\\):\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{32 \\pm \\sqrt{1024 - 768}}{2} = \\frac{32 \\pm \\sqrt{256}}{2} = \\frac{32 \\pm 16}{2}\n\\]\n\nThus, the roots are:\n\n\\[\nt = 24 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = 8\n\\]\n\nSo, \\(x = 24\\) and \\(y = 8\\). Therefore:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{32 + 16\\sqrt{3}} = \\sqrt{24} + \\sqrt{8} = 2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2} = 2(\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2})\n\\]\n\nNext, substitute back into the original expression:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8 + 2(\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2})}\n\\]\n\nWe simplify further:\n\n\\[\n8 + 2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nWe assume the form \\(\\sqrt{8 + 2\\sqrt{6} + 2\\sqrt{2}} = a \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{b}\\). We need to recognize a trigonometric identity. Consider:\n\n\\[\n\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\cos (15^\\circ) = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n2 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nSo,\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8 + 2(\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2})} = \\sqrt{8 + 2 \\cdot 2 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12}} = \\sqrt{8 + 4 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12}}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\),\n\n\\[\n4 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8 + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}} = 2 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nGiven \\(\\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12} = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}\\),\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{8 + \\sqrt{32 + \\sqrt{768}}} = 2 \\cos \\frac{\\pi}{12}\n\\]\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(2, 12)}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(2, 12)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
